#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134698} :activity/id #uuid "dac16d4a-58b8-5e9c-b569-e42a4c036b80" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049557 :topic/id #uuid "fee59c68-0ad2-5141-b297-31cf8632cc46" :topic/math-academy-id 2167} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134698 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135060 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following inconsistent system of equations:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince the system is inconsistent, we cannot find an exact solution. However, as we've previously seen, we can attempt to find the so-called least-squares solution, which is a vector ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\|  A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible. \n\nAs before, the least-squares solution can be found by solving the corresponding normal equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, note the following: \n\n- Previously, we solved the normal equation by left-multiplying the normal equation by $(A^T A)^{-1}.$\n\n- However, in this case, the second column of $A$ is a multiple of the first.\n\n- So, the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent.\n\n- Therefore, $A^T A$ is not invertible.\n\nTo solve the normal equation in this case, we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5 & -20 \\\\ -20 & 80\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the last equation by Gaussian elimination, we obtain the general solution\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1+4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that our solution is not unique! The second term of the least-squares solution (highlighted in blue) represents vectors from the null space of $A.$ \n\nIn general, if a matrix $A$ has collinear columns, then the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf x = \\mathbf b$ is not unique." :tutorial/id #uuid "2f2e227a-264e-5e00-8c12-ea534f4b351f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3637 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "73e4890e-8287-5a80-8c3c-2c95ce05512e" :step/math-academy-id 20272 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134733}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134699 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133801 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133610 :question/id #uuid "cb31a55d-6107-509a-80e2-9bdffcaff6e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5590"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050178}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269136} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326896} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367436} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368554}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"} :step/id #uuid "2664b077-4f38-5154-af4c-d4fd21596b27" :step/math-academy-id 20273 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134700}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134700 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133802 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133611 :question/id #uuid "c5097abf-c541-5c52-85d3-1e56a4e53cb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5591"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269140} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367450}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"} :step/id #uuid "f4594719-c6f8-53f5-bc1a-22e17b863c14" :step/math-academy-id 20275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134733 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133822 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133634 :question/id #uuid "8a39f109-9436-55f8-bbb8-87253668378d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5963"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fda08f5d-4513-5be0-b8c4-475e82f9bf5a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048284}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269135} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367429}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Normal Equation Corresponding to a Linear System"} :step/id #uuid "a43860da-1a59-5a1c-930e-23d6f893c13a" :step/math-academy-id 22147 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134699}}] :activity/title "The Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System (With Collinearity)" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134027}]}
